{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1553805-eidectic-memory-question","id":1553805,"title":"Eidectic Memory question","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Vampire: The Masquerade"],"posts":[{"date":"2025-09-13T20:04:22-07:00","id":1553805,"title":"Eidectic Memory question","content":"I have a player who is trying to exploit the merit Eidectic Memory by claiming that it lets him remember everything that he's ever seen or heard and expects not to have to keep notes. Is there a book that explains the merit better than just a simple overview? I told him that the merit is basically photographic memoey and that even RL people who have this don't recall everything (a simple Google search shows that). Any suggestions on how to deal with this? Thanks.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24119-alucardtes","name":"Alucardtes","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar24119_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"36"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-09-13T20:32:46-07:00","id":1553808,"title":"","content":"Kinda depends on what edition we're talking about.<br /> <br /> In V20, there's a roll for memories being formed under high stress situations; which could be asked for retroactively.<br /> <br /> As for &quot;having to keep notes?&quot; That's nothing the game is going to solve for you. If you have a character is obnoxiously good memory (perfect or not) in your game, it's ultimately on you to make sure this is a fun part of the game for the player. They paid the points for it, and you let it. It's not &quot;exploiting&quot; anything to want your character to know things you paid character points to know.<br /> <br /> I can sympathize with this being frustrating, but if you want the player to take a large role in managing a trait like this, you need to set those expectations during character creation.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-09-14T00:19:29-07:00","id":1553809,"title":"","content":"As Heavy Arms said, it is edition-based, mostly<br /> <br /> <b>Eidetic Memory</b> (2pt. Merit) You remember what you see and hear with perfect detail. Documents, events and conversations, can be committed to memory with only minor concentration. Under stressful conditions involving numerous distractions, you must make a Perception + Alertness roll (difficulty 6) to summon enough concentration to absorb what your senses detected.<br /> <br /> So, while if fustrating, your player indeed &quot;can take notes&quot;<br /> It is better to talk it over your player, or if yo uasks me, give some flavor to it<br /> A vampire is already &quot;stuck in the past&quot; thanks to the Embrace, even if they change phisically, tattoo, cut down hair, etc, they simply &quot;resets&quot; next day, it is not a stretch their mind in a sense &quot;stuck&quot; in a present and the past lives in them in their memories<br /> On the other hand this could be , with a crude word, &quot;exploited&quot; not by the player but the Storyteller; Vampires are forever tormented by their Beast, their withering Humanity and their crumbling past and even humans have memory-flashbacks very often, a smell, a picture, a situation, a sound, tons of things could remind us involuntary things we might not really want to &quot;relive&quot;<br /> And a Cainite that have no choice but <i>remember</i>?<br /> It is a great tool to emphasize the Personal Horror aspect of the game, it is a constant, ever present reminder what they have<i> lost </i>and they don't have the luxury to forget<br /> They will <i>never</i> forget<br /> They will never forget the last kiss, the warmth of the sun, the laughter of their child, the smile of their friends, the hugg... never<br /> But the world slowly turns to dust around them, the child grows up, even forget them, frirends grew old... close ones will die<br /> Or died, and they realize, jesus they <i>died </i>it is just an echo in their mind and the Beast laughs<br /> And with every passing day it will be worse and he already have a liftime worth of memories and it will be more and more and more... <br /> <br /> The game is for fun, we play to have fun<br /> This is the ultimate turth of the day, never forget this<br /> So let them have their fun I say, but be open to the possibility to grab their balls and squeeze it until ltheir eyes pop out - it is a game, yes, a game of personal horror<br /> And mertis and flaws could be and 99% of the time are the best source of that horror<br /> <br /> Many don't relaize what it is means to have a perfect memory, what it is means to <i>remember</i> and simply unable to forget<br /> Immortality isn't living forever. That's not what it feels like. Immortality is everybody else dying.<br /> And for an immortal who is robbed of the gift to be able to forget? Cursed with the ability that they can't turn of to perfectly remember everything? <br /> Yes, it is a merit, with a cute little benefit, but in reality in the hands of the ST? You are for a treat!<br /> <br /> Anyway, if it bothers you your player takes notes and remember details, you can always tweak your story to minimalize the effect of such clarity, one where details are not that important or make them important so your player could feel themself essential part of the coterie and story to balance out situations where the emphasis is on their shortcomings (eg, maybe they have a perfect memory but when it comes to social interactions they are a blundering fool and in an Elysium perfect memory or not, they are simply the plaything of the Harpies and other social predators or in combatthey have to be protected because they are squishy)<br /> <br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23890-shadeprowler","name":"Shadeprowler","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23890_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"928"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-09-14T19:37:13-07:00","id":1553825,"title":"","content":"Overall your player is right. There are possible limitations to it depending on edition, but the core assumption is exactly what the player said: the character is expected to remember by default. They shouldn't be penalized just because the player isn't taking notes, unless you actually forbids other players from taking notes.<br /> <br /> Even the limitations <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/\">Heavy Arms</a> and <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/\">Shadeprowler</a> cited are there for narrative reasons. They're there to prevent a perfect memory coming in the way of a good story, and they should be exceptions, not the rule, otherwise you're punishing the player for choosing this trait. You should, by all means, remind the player of anything the character ever experienced, even retelling scenes if this is reasonable.<br /> <br /> Now, game management is something else entirely. If you need the player to take notes so the game flows better, you need the player to take notes and that's it. I usually have a lot on my hands and I ask my players to take notes so we have everything at hand when I forget something myself. When someone takes Eidetic Memory I do ask them to keep careful notes as a player, just to ease my work.<br /> <br /> If the lack of notes is frustrating, talk to them. The trait is there to help the character, not necessarily the player. If it really annoys you, you may even ask them to change it for something else, no ST is required to allow traits they find disruptive to their style. That said, as long as the character has it you shouldn't be stingy with information without reason.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-09-14T23:50:39-07:00","id":1553832,"title":"","content":"If the player expects the Merit means you, the ST, to do all the work so he doesn't have to, he is wrong. The Merit is about the character. It is not about the player or ST.<br /> <br /> As ST, you can certainly help the player out if he can't remember and you can. But the Merit doesn't force the OOC work onto the ST because the player is too lazy or simply has a bad memory.<br /> <br /> If you, as the ST, have certain limits, the player can't expect the Merit to force you to do extra work. He either has to accept the limitations or put in the extra work OOC. If he wants more than what you can (reasonably) provide, it is up to him to rise to the challenge.<br /> <br /> &quot;Expects not to have to keep notes&quot; is definitely an OOC demand, and that is not what the Merit implies.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}